```json
[
  {
    "stratum": "Strategy / Commitment",
    "text": "My family has run a mid-size furniture manufacturing business for three generations, currently doing about $14M in annual revenue with 60 employees in Ohio. We've been approached by a large European retailer offering an exclusive licensing deal: they'd pay us $2M upfront plus royalties to produce a line under their brand for their 200+ stores, but we'd need to dedicate roughly 70% of our production capacity to them for a five-year contract, effectively locking out most of our existing wholesale accounts. Separately, we have a term sheet from a regional bank for a $3M expansion loan to open a second facility in North Carolina, letting us keep our existing customer base while adding capacity for new ones — but this means taking on significant debt and a multi-year build-out with no guaranteed new customers lined up yet. The licensing deal offers certainty and cash now but ties us to one partner's fortunes and could damage relationships with wholesalers we've served for decades if they feel abandoned. The expansion path keeps our independence but is riskier and slower to pay off. Both offers expire in five weeks — the retailer needs an answer to lock in their catalog planning, and the bank's rate lock ends the same week. We can't pursue both; the licensing deal's capacity commitment would make the expansion loan's underlying business case fall apart. Which direction should we commit to?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Strategy / Commitment",
    "text": "I run a 45-person marketing agency that currently serves a mix of clients across retail, hospitality, and healthcare. Over the last two years, our healthcare accounts have grown from 20% to 55% of revenue, and we've built genuine expertise there — case studies, referral relationships, a nurse-turned-strategist on staff. A large health system has now offered us a three-year retainer that would require us to go fully exclusive to healthcare, dropping our retail and hospitality clients (about $1.8M combined annual revenue) within six months to avoid conflicts of interest, since the health system considers some of our current clients competitors of their partners. Going this route means rebranding as a healthcare-only shop, renegotiating or losing eight existing client relationships, and betting the firm's future on one sector and largely one client relationship, though the retainer itself is worth more than what we'd give up. The alternative is to decline, stay a generalist agency, and continue building healthcare work alongside everything else, growing more slowly but keeping diversification. Once we tell current retail and hospitality clients we're exiting their sector, those relationships won't come back if the healthcare bet doesn't work out, and the health system wants a signed exclusivity agreement within three weeks. Which path should we commit to?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Operations / Execution",
    "text": "Our hospital network has already approved consolidating three emergency departments into one expanded regional ER, a decision made by the board six months ago after the standalone ERs were losing money and duplicating specialist coverage. The new facility will have 40 beds versus the combined 55 across the three current sites, but will run more efficiently with centralized imaging and trauma staff. We need to close the three existing ERs and transition all emergency intake to the new site, but we can't do it all at once: the new building's final inspection and staffing hires (22 additional nurses, 6 physicians) won't be complete for four months, current ER staff are on contracts that require 60-day transfer notice, and two of the three existing sites serve areas with the region's only ambulance stations, meaning any gap in coverage risks response-time failures. We also need to migrate patient records and coordinate with 14 contracted ambulance services who need advance notice of routing changes. Leadership wants the full transition done within seven months to stop the financial bleeding from operating three sites. Given these constraints — staffing lead times, construction completion, ambulance rerouting, and the need to avoid any period with reduced total bed capacity — how should we sequence the closures and the ramp-up of the new site?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Operations / Execution",
    "text": "We've committed to replacing the warehouse management system across all five of our fulfillment centers this year — leadership decided this after last year's peak season saw pick errors cost us $1.2M in returns and mischipped orders, and the new system has already been purchased and piloted successfully at one small site. Each warehouse ships between 8,000 and 40,000 orders a day, and the switchover requires roughly 4 days of reduced capacity (about 60% of normal throughput) while staff retrain and the new barcode scanners get installed. We cannot take more than one large warehouse offline at a time without breaching contractual delivery guarantees to our three biggest retail partners, and our busiest warehouse (40,000 orders/day, serving the West Coast) can only tolerate the changeover in a specific 10-day window in March before its regional peak season starts. We have 14 trained implementation staff who can be deployed to help each site, but each deployment ties them up for about a week including travel and cleanup. Two of the five sites also share a regional trucking contract, so if we take a truck route offline for one changeover it affects the other site's outbound schedule that week. Given these throughput limits, staffing availability, and the West Coast site's fixed window, how should we sequence the five rollouts across the year?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Brand / Creative",
    "text": "My craft brewery just completed an acquisition of a smaller regional brewery known for a very different style — they make traditional lagers with old-world branding (gothic lettering, a heritage crest, muted colors) aimed at an older, loyal local drinker base, while my brewery makes experimental sours and hazy IPAs with bright, illustrated cans aimed at younger drinkers who follow us on social media. Financially the deal makes sense: combined we get their bottling line and distribution routes I've needed for two years. But we now have to decide how the combined company presents itself. Their fans are vocal about not wanting to be \"absorbed\" into a brand they see as trendy and disposable, and mine are already joking online about the old-fashioned lager label looking out of place next to our cans on shelf. We could keep both breweries operating under separate identities with no visible connection, merge into a single new name and visual identity for everything going forward, or keep both names but visibly signal they're part of one family (shared tagline, shared taproom design cues) so we get some cross-promotional benefit without forcing either audience to accept the other's aesthetic. Retail buyers have asked us to clarify our shelf presentation before the fall order cycle, which locks in three months from now. How should we present the combined identity?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Brand / Creative",
    "text": "I run a nonprofit that has produced after-school science curriculum for elementary kids (ages 6-10) for the last twelve years, with a friendly, cartoonish visual identity and simple, playful language. Last year we won a grant to expand into middle school (ages 11-13), and pilot feedback has been mixed: several teachers said the material \"feels babyish\" to seventh graders even when the content itself is appropriately advanced, while our long-time elementary partner schools specifically cite the playful tone and characters as why kids stay engaged. We now need to decide how our materials, website, and printed workbooks present themselves going forward, since we're producing next year's full catalog in the next six weeks and can't easily maintain two entirely separate visual universes given our three-person design team's capacity. We could keep one unified brand voice across both age groups and accept some friction with older kids, split into two visibly distinct sub-brands under one parent name with different tone and visual language for each age band, or redesign a single voice that ages up the whole product line and risk losing some of what makes the younger material work. Teachers who reviewed the pilot say they want clarity before back-to-school orders open. How should we handle the voice and visual identity across our age ranges?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Evidence Interpretation",
    "text": "Our fitness app's monthly churn dropped from 9.5% to 6.2% over the last quarter, and I need to understand what actually drove that before we decide anything about scaling either change. During that same quarter we made two changes simultaneously: we cut the monthly subscription price from $19.99 to $14.99 for all new sign-ups, and we rolled out a new onboarding flow that walks users through setting a specific weekly goal in their first session instead of dropping them straight into the workout library. The cohort data shows: users who signed up before the price cut and never saw the new onboarding had churn of 9.1% (basically flat versus history). Users who signed up after both changes went live had churn of 5.8%. But we also have a small group — about 400 users — who signed up during a two-week window after the onboarding change shipped but before the price cut took effect, due to a staggered regional rollout, and their churn was 6.4%, nearly identical to the full post-launch group. Our total post-launch cohort is about 11,000 users, while the onboarding-only cohort is much smaller and drawn from a single region that historically has had somewhat better retention than our other markets anyway. Marketing wants to claim the price cut drove the improvement to justify a further discount; product wants to claim onboarding did, to justify investing in more onboarding work instead. What does this data actually show about which change is responsible?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Evidence Interpretation",
    "text": "Our auto parts plant's defect rate on a key bracket component dropped from 3.8% to 1.4% over the last two months, and I've gotten two conflicting internal reports about why, which I need to make sense of before we decide whether to expand either change to our other three product lines. The QA team's report attributes the improvement almost entirely to a supplier change we made in the same period — we switched to a new steel supplier with tighter tolerance certification, and QA notes that the metal composition tests show meaningfully less variance than the old supplier's stock. The operations team's report instead credits a machine calibration change they implemented in the same two-month window, adjusting the stamping press pressure settings, and points out that defect rates started dropping in the first two weeks, before the new steel supplier's shipments had even fully replaced the old stock in our inventory (we were still working through old stock for the first three weeks). However, I also know that the two teams pulled defect data from slightly different sample windows — QA measured from the date the new steel was approved for use, while operations measured from the date the calibration change was implemented, which was eleven days earlier. Both reports show real improvement in their respective windows, and neither team tested their change in isolation before rolling both out. Given this, what can we actually conclude about which change is responsible for the defect reduction, and is the evidence here sufficient to justify rolling either change out plant-wide?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Product / Service Design",
    "text": "I own a boutique gym with about 500 active members and want to launch a personal training subscription to add a new revenue stream, since our group class revenue has plateaued and members keep asking for more individualized attention. We have four trainers with capacity for roughly 25 combined weekly training sessions beyond their current class-teaching load, and I want a subscription structure rather than pay-per-session, since recurring revenue is what our investor is pushing for ahead of a possible second location next year. Members range from casual gym-goers who want occasional form checks to serious lifters who want dedicated programming and weekly check-ins, and our current flat membership is $89/month with no tiering at all. I'm considering whether to offer a single mid-priced tier bundling a fixed number of sessions plus app-based check-ins, versus multiple tiers ranging from a low-cost \"programming only\" option (no in-person sessions, just a custom plan and monthly check-in) up to a premium tier with weekly one-on-one sessions, versus an à la carte credit system where members buy blocks of training credits usable however they like. Whatever we choose needs to fit within our four trainers' 25-session weekly capacity without over-promising availability, and I want to launch this within six weeks to catch our usual post-New Year's sign-up surge. What should this subscription actually include, and how should it be structured?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Product / Service Design",
    "text": "I manage product for a community bank with about $800M in assets, and we want to launch a new checking account product aimed at small business owners, since our current small business offering is essentially a personal checking account with a different name and we're losing sign-ups to fintech competitors offering integrated invoicing and expense tools. We have the technical capacity to build a mobile app add-on layer through our existing core banking vendor within four months, but we can't build a full accounting suite ourselves — our engineering team is three people. Our target customers, based on interviews with 30 existing small business clients, are mostly service businesses (contractors, consultants, small retailers) with fewer than 10 employees, and their most common complaints are having to log into three separate systems to reconcile invoices, payments, and payroll, plus wanting faster access to deposited funds than our standard two-day hold. We're deciding what this account should actually include: whether to build a lightweight invoicing tool ourselves, partner with an existing invoicing app via API integration, offer faster funds access as the primary differentiator instead of chasing software features at all, or bundle a flat monthly fee against a small transaction-based fee structure. We also need to decide whether to offer this as a single account type or multiple tiers based on business size. Our board wants a defined product scope within six weeks to greenlight the build. What should this account's scope and structure be?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Finance / Resource Allocation",
    "text": "Our school district has a one-time $2.1 million discretionary fund from a state surplus reallocation, and I have three competing requests that together total $3.4 million, so I need to decide how to split the $2.1 million we actually have. The technology department wants $1.2 million to replace aging classroom computers across all six schools, arguing that 40% of current devices are past manufacturer support and failing weekly, disrupting instruction. The special education department wants $900,000 to hire eight additional teacher's aides for the next two years, citing state-mandated student-to-aide ratios we're currently out of compliance with at three schools, which carries risk of a formal citation. Facilities wants $1.3 million for roof repairs at our oldest elementary school, where two classrooms had to be closed this winter due to leaks and the district's insurer has flagged the building as a rising liability risk. All three needs are documented and real; none can be fully funded from other budget lines, and this state surplus money cannot be carried forward or combined with next year's regular budget cycle, so whatever isn't allocated now reverts to the state. The school board meets in ten days and needs a recommended allocation across these three claims. How should the $2.1 million be divided?"
  },
  {
    "stratum": "Finance / Resource Allocation",
    "text": "My startup just closed a funding round that lets us hire 10 new engineers this quarter, and I need to decide how to split those 10 hires across our three engineering teams: platform, mobile, and data. Platform (currently 8 engineers) is the foundation everything else depends on and has been running with significant technical debt that's starting to cause production incidents — roughly two per week now, up from near-zero six months ago. Mobile (currently 5 engineers) is under pressure to ship a redesigned app that a major partner has conditioned a renewed distribution deal on, with a deadline in four months. Data (currently 3 engineers) is the smallest team and has been unable to keep up with requests from sales and product for basic reporting, causing recurring friction, but has no external deadline attached to it. All three team leads have made a case for at least 4 of the 10 hires, which is obviously more than the total available, and I can't realistically split hires mid-quarter once onboarding starts since ramp-up takes about six weeks and reassigning people afterward causes real disruption. I need to decide the allocation before we start extending offers next week. How should the 10 new engineering hires be split across the three teams?"
  }
]
```
